    "response": "In short: **no, you should not make something of the fact that the panel was made up entirely of women.** It is very unlikely to be a sign of unfair bias against you, and raising it is almost certain to be counterproductive.\n\nHere is a breakdown of why, along with an explanation of how academic hiring decisions typically work in this situation and what your best next steps are.\n\n---\n\n### 1. What the panel's composition actually means\n* **Not evidence of gender discrimination:** Because you, the other shortlisted candidates, and the successful candidate are all women, gender preference did not disadvantage you in favor of another gender. \n* **How panels are formed:** Academic panels are usually formed based on designated roles (e.g., Head of Department, Director of Teaching, subject lead, HR representative). In many economics and business departments, committee assignments simply reflect whoever holds those administrative posts at the time or who was available on the interview dates.\n* **Institutional policy nuances:** Some universities have explicit recruitment policies stating that panels must be gender-balanced (e.g., requiring at least one man and one woman). However, even if an internal procedural guideline like this was overlooked, an all-women panel on its own does not signify bias against an individual woman candidate.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why the other candidate may have been chosen despite your publication record\nIt is understandably frustrating to see a candidate who has not yet finished their PhD and has fewer publications get selected over someone with a completed doctorate, six papers, and four years of teaching. However, in lecturer appointments at public universities, hiring decisions frequently pivot on factors beyond publication count:\n\n* **Strategic fit & role focus:** \"Lecturer\" roles often prioritize teaching, curriculum development, and student employability over pure research output. If the department is looking to launch applied programs, teach professional or policy-oriented modules, or connect students with internships, **\"strong industry experience\"** can be a decisive asset.\n* **Specific research or teaching niche:** The successful candidate’s specific expertise may have plugged an exact gap in the department’s current timetable or strategic plan that your profile, despite its strength, did not.\n* **The interview and teaching presentation:** Once candidates reach the final shortlist of three, baseline qualifications have already been established. The final rank usually comes down to the teaching demonstration, the job talk, and how well the candidate articulated their vision for the specific department's needs.\n* **Trajectory vs. track record:** Panels sometimes weigh perceived \"future trajectory\" and specific grant-capture potential alongside completed work.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The two-week delay in feedback\n* **Academic HR is notoriously slow:** Two weeks is rarely a sign of avoidance. In a public university, written feedback typically cannot be sent until the preferred candidate has formally signed their contract, committee minutes have been approved by HR, and the hiring chair has time to draft formal comments.\n* A 2-to-4-week turnaround for formal written feedback is common across higher education.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Do not mention the gender of the panel:** Raising the panel’s gender composition would likely be viewed as defensive or unfounded, and it could burn bridges with colleagues in your disciplinary network.\n2. **Follow up politely on feedback:** If you haven’t heard back after three weeks from your initial request, send a brief, courteous follow-up to the HR coordinator or panel chair:\n   > *\"I understand the department has many commitments at this point in the term. I remains very interested in any constructive feedback the panel might share regarding my interview and application to help me in my future career development.\"*\n3. **Focus purely on actionable criteria:** When the feedback arrives, focus on what specific areas (e.g., teaching demo, research alignment, industry relevance) separated the top candidates. This will give you clarity on whether the decision was driven by a specific programmatic need rather than any deficiency in your academic record.\n",
